der’s equation:"},{"x":0.50458,"y":0.48307,"w":0.22684,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"x2 + y2 = (cos t)2 + (sin t)2 = 1."},{"x":0.21476,"y":0.48806,"w":0.03121,"h":0.01564,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"t = p"},{"x":0.13718,"y":0.50956,"w":0.0665,"h":0.01822,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"P t"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.51057,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"The curve rises as the k-component z = t increases. Each time t increases by 2p, the curve completes one turn around the cylinder. The curve is called a helix (from an old Greek word for “spiral”). The equations"},{"x":0.10737,"y":0.5302,"w":0.11798,"h":0.01461,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"x2 + y2 = 1 t = 0"},{"x":0.04963,"y":0.53137,"w":0.04143,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(1, 0, 0)"},{"x":0.5033,"y":0.56428,"w":0.22911,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"x = cos t, y = sin t, z = t"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.58602,"w":0.59309,"h":0.04544,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"parametrize the helix, the interval -q 6 t 6 q being understood. Figure 13.4 shows more helices. Note how constant multiples of the parameter t can change the number of turns per unit of time."},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.59154,"w":0.23102,"h":0.04041,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 13.3 The upper half of the helix r(t) = (cos t)i + (sin t)j + tk (Example 1)."},{"x":0.22807,"y":0.83275,"w":0.15567,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r(t) = (cos t)i + (sin t)j + tk"},{"x":0.47424,"y":0.83358,"w":0.17203,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r(t) = (cos t)i + (sin t)j + 0.3tk"},{"x":0.72991,"y":0.83394,"w":0.16941,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r(t) = (cos 5t)i + (sin 5t)j + tk"},{"x":0.21371,"y":0.85457,"w":0.45716,"h":0.01228,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 13.4 Helices spiral upward around a cylinder, like coiled springs."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.88285,"w":0.16512,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Limits and Continuity"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.90751,"w":0.5929,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The way we define limits of vector-valued functions is similar to the way we define limits of real-valued functions."}]}